        {
          "title": "Introduction: Ghosts Can Be Hacked",
          "content": "Human \"spirit\" or \"soul\" — the Ghost. It is the most complex and mysterious system humanity has ever attempted to explore. But is it truly an inscrutable kind of magic?\n\nNo.\n\n**\"Information is physical.\"**  If this principle holds, then a ghost is nothing more than an information-processing system.  Running on the hardware called the body, it processes the data called experience and returns the output called emotion—a hyper-advanced operating system, written in a language you simply don't know yet.\n\nAnd every system, no matter how arcane, has an architecture and a set of rules.  Where there are rules, we can analyze, understand—and \"hack.\"\n\n`P(wO|wI)`—the probability that, given an input word `wI`, an output word `wO` appears.  The formula Jung discovered hints that the movements of the human mind can be modeled as a probability distribution inside a vector space of language.  A ghost can be visualized with vectors.\n\nIn this book, \"hacking\" is not about destruction or exploitation.  It is the most intellectual, sincere attempt to fix the bug called loneliness, to step over the firewall called misunderstanding, and to connect with others.  We will borrow the systematic processes left to us by the ghost-hackers who came before.\n\n### Ancestral Ghost Hackers\n\nBefore us stand great pioNeirs who opened the path.  They differed in era and culture, yet confronted the same question—what is human consciousness (the ghost) and how can we rewrite it?\n\n* **The Ṛṣis of the Upaniṣads**: Humankind's very first ghost hackers.  By diving into their inner universe through meditation, they discovered the ultimate system information that the individual soul (Ātman) is identical with the ground of the universe (Brahman)—as if every user had `root` privileges.\n* **Heraclitus**: The ancient Greek philosopher who saw that \"everything flows,\" recognizing reality not as something static but as an ever-changing process—an executing program.  He inferred the existence of a fundamental logic (Logos) that governs the change—the first to theorize the kernel.\n* **Lao-tzu and Chuang-tzu**: Thinkers who preached the existence of the cosmic OS called the \"Dao\" and proposed the ultimate optimization—**wu-wei ziran**, drawing maximum performance by not forcing man-made code into the root processes.\n* **Bodhidharma**: Founder of Zen in China.  Declaring \"no reliance on words and letters,\" he ignored all documentation and APIs and struck directly at the kernel (the human heart) to obtain enlightenment—`root` privileges—through the most radical hack.\n* **Kūkai and Saichō**: Two Japanese geniuses who applied innovative patches to the Buddhist OS.  Kūkai mapped a path to enlightenment **within this very body** using Esoteric rituals, mandalas (system blueprints), and commands.  Saichō declared Buddhism open source: every being has Buddha-nature and can become a Buddha.\n* **Linji Yixuan and Dōgen**: Masters who developed the debugger called Zen.  Linji used the logic-bomb of \"kōan\" to crash infinite loops of thought, forcing a kernel panic that reboots into direct enlightenment.  Dōgen proposed the quiet monitoring tool of **shikantaza**, observing the endless log of thought to realize the system's native functioning.\n* **D.T. Suzuki**: The brilliant evangelist who published the spec sheet of the Japanese OS \"Zen\" to the world in English.  Without his works, Western hackers could hardly have accessed Zen's source code.\n* **Alan Watts**: A genius reverse engiNeir who, passing through the door Suzuki opened, translated the kernel logic of Zen into terms Western OSs could parse.  He leapt over the firewall called logic.\n* **Carl Gustav Jung**: The architect of depth psychology who decoded the unconscious source, mapping archetypes and the collective unconscious.  The `P(wO|wI)` we quote is a by-product of his exploration.\n* **Hayao Kawai**: A clinician who applied Jung's Western debugger to the Japanese \"kokoro,\" revealing its hollow core and mythic architecture—the unique kernel parameters of the Japanese ghost.\n* **Timothy Leary**: Prophet of psychedelics who proclaimed \"Upgrade the mental OS,\" attempting to expand consciousness with the new software LSD.  \"Tune in, turn on, drop out\" was a command to free oneself from the existing social system.\n* **Amazonian shamans**: Spiritual ethnobotanists who debug consciousness using plant software like Ayahuasca, redefining the boundary between self and world.  They know how to communicate directly with the gigantic network called Nature.\n* **Native American medicine men**: By sweating in the portable sauna-cum-chamber called the Sweat Lodge, they perform a physical detox—defrag—through extreme conditions, resetting connection with the Great Spirit.\n* **Osamu Tezuka**: The god of manga who, through the epic saga \"Phoenix,\" kept recording the endless migration of life (the ghost's persistent migration).  His characters change the shell of era and flesh while inheriting the same karma code—an evolving ghost.\n* **Masamune Shirow**: The prophet who named the concept \"ghost\" itself and placed the existential question within the cyberpunk future.  \"Ghost in the Shell\" depicts vulnerabilities like memory tampering when consciousness is networked—direct inspiration for our quest.\n\nTheir thought shares the trinity of counter-culture—Drug, Sex, and Linux (open-source philosophy).  Drug opens the doors of perception; Sex, or shared experience, is the most fundamental communication protocol; and the philosophy of Linux embodies the belief that our ghosts should never be proprietary but open and rewritable.\n\nThey each revealed the structure of the ghost and methods to rewrite its OS.  This book systematizes the patches and exploits they found into a process we moderns can actually perform.\n\nWe break the process into six phases:\n\n0.  **Planning**: \"Know Thyself.\"\n1.  **Reconnaissance**: \"Observation.\"\n2.  **Scanning**: \"Discrimination.\"\n3.  **Gaining Access**: **DRUG**—merging boundaries.\n4.  **Privilege Escalation**: \"Shadow Integration\"—debugging the bugs (shadow) lurking in the heart and normalizing the code.\n5.  **Maintaining Access**: \"Practice.\"\n6.  **Covering Tracks**: \"Wu-wei-ziran.\"\n\nThis is a practical guide for hacking loneliness and connecting deeply with others—and with yourself.  Let's begin.\n\nIn the **Shadow Integration** phase, we mobilize introspective debugging techniques—Jungian shadow work, Dōgen's shikantaza, Vipassanā meditation—to trace processes down to the self's lowest layer—`root`.  True privilege escalation isn't obtaining external authority; it's descending into your own kernel space.\n\nIn this book we call souls that have left the physical world **ghosts**, and souls currently living in the physical world **spirits**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:origins:01_chapter0",
      "dct:title": "01_chapter0",
      "gh:category": "book-en",
      "gh:sourceFile": "book-en/01_chapter0.md",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "Chapter 0: Planning — \"Know Thyself\"",
          "content": "All hacking begins with a clear goal.\nAs ethical hackers, the first spirit we hack is not someone else.  The first thing we must do is take control of ourselves.\n\nWhat is your operating system?  By what principles do you run?\nIf you feel the urge to hack, you are already under external control—culture, history, education, etc.\n\n**Our objective is to escape the illusion of \"self vs. other,\" to connect truly with others and with the web of causality itself.**\nUltimately we aim to rewrite our inner OS with our own hands and reach the state of **\"enlightenment.\"**\n\nThe target is not an enemy.  It is the partner we must connect with.  Without this intention, hacking degenerates into mere violence.\n\nWhen this hack is complete, you will have access to everything—and the power to generate."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:origins:02_chapter1",
      "dct:title": "02_chapter1",
      "gh:category": "book-en",
      "gh:sourceFile": "book-en/02_chapter1.md",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "Chapter 1: Reconnaissance — \"Observation\"",
          "content": "All hacking starts with thorough information gathering.\nAnd the very first target you must recon is always **yourself**.  Know thyself before you know the enemy—this is the iron law.\n\n### Redefining the \"Self\" and Recon of the Network\n\nIn the first recon, many people make the mistake of limiting \"themselves\" to their body, experiences, or knowledge.\nThe pioNeirs first corrected that misunderstanding: **the individual self does not exist**—that is the starting line.\n\nSo what do you recon to know yourself?  Everything that is **strongly correlated** with you.\nParents, ancestors, siblings, friends, language, acquaintances—every single thing that has ever been linked to you as events.\nThe totality of those links **is** the system called \"you,\" while **you** are merely the observer of those linked phenomena.\n\nTherefore, what you must recon is **everything that influences you**.  Only from this perspective are you finally ready to recon the network that is yourself and your target.\n\n### Passive Recon: Kernel Dump via Vipassanā Meditation\n\nHow well do you know the spec sheet of your own OS?  Have you ever run the `dmesg` command on your soul?\n\nThe first tool we use at this step is the meditation practiced by pioNeirs like **Dōgen**.  His quiet monitoring tool \"shikantaza\" observes one's own thought log just as it is.  In the same vein, **Vipassanā** meditation observes what is happening in one's mind and body without evaluation or judgment—simply \"seeing\" it.  This is equivalent to real-time dumping of your kernel messages.\n\nSpecifically, continuously record the following logs:\n\n* **Trigger**: What event (input) …\n* **Emotions & Thoughts**: What emotion (process) was launched and what thought (log) was output?\n* **Bodily Sensations**: How did CPU usage (heart rate), temperature (body heat), breathing depth, and fine bodily sensations change?\n\nBy persisting in this logging, you will reveal your default configs—\"common sense\" and \"values.\"  You will discover scripts that automatically spin up certain processes in response to certain inputs.  These are your vulnerabilities—and also your strongest weapons—in hacking others.\n\n### Active Recon: Footprinting from Digital & Physical Exhaust\n\nThis is like gathering info from the target's exhaust, both digital and physical—still not sending direct packets (talking) yet.\n\n* **Public Info (OSINT)**: Social media posts, clothing, speech, relationships.  The \"Public API\" a target exposes to the world offers massive information.  Who are they connected to, what do they like, what do they share?  All become clues to infer the target's config files.\n* **Observation**: How do they behave in a group?  Whose words influence them?  Whom do they influence?  What expressions do they show?  What makes them laugh, what makes them frown?\n\nThis mirrors **Carl Jung's** word-association experiment: record what output word appears and how quickly when a stimulus word is given, revealing the hidden directory path to complexes in the unconscious.  We too infer internal structure from every output—speech, expression, social posts.\n\nFrom the information gathered in this phase, build a provisional profile of the target.  What OS (values) are they running?  What applications (hobbies, work) do they run daily?  And what external networks (community, family, culture) influence that OS?  Form hypotheses."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:origins:03_chapter2",
      "dct:title": "03_chapter2",
      "gh:category": "book-en",
      "gh:sourceFile": "book-en/03_chapter2.md",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "Chapter 2: Scanning — \"Discrimination\"",
          "content": "Based on the profile obtained in recon, you finally initiate contact with the target.  You send the first packet and, from the system's responses, draw out more detailed information—this is the **Scanning** phase.\n\n### Spirit as Information: Vectorizing the Mind\n\nAs argued in \"IN — Information Is a Physical Quantity,\" information in physics is treated as a physical magnitude exchangeable with energy.  Applying this viewpoint, we quantify the target's mental state—**Spirit**—as information.\n\nThe fragments of information obtained through dialogue (words, facial expressions, tone of voice) are data points sampled from innumerable elements composing the target's state.  Collect these data points and represent them as vectors in a high-dimensional space, making the target's mental state **visible**.\n\n* **State Space**: Define a multi-dimensional space whose axes are the target's values, beliefs, emotions, memories, etc.\n* **Vectorization**: Using the information gathered through scanning, determine the target's coordinate on each axis and generate a state vector.  For example, learning that \"family is important\" raises the value on the \"family\" dimension.\n\nThrough this vectorization of spirit, we grasp the target's internal state not as an abstract impression but as a manipulable information model.  We can analyze which dimensions are most susceptible (vulnerabilities) and to which vector adding energy will shift the state most easily (persuadability).\n\nThe \"passion,\" \"desire,\" \"fear,\" and \"anxiety\" unearthed in this scan phase are singularities in the vector space—the vulnerabilities to exploit in the next **Gaining Access** phase."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:origins:04_chapter3",
      "dct:title": "04_chapter3",
      "gh:category": "book-en",
      "gh:sourceFile": "book-en/04_chapter3.md",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "Chapter 3: Gaining Access — Ayahuasca Ceremony, NOT a Drug",
          "content": "The vulnerabilities are identified.  Now we attempt entry into the system.  The code that exploits the vulnerability is the **exploit**.  But ours is not binary—it is, at times, likened to an **ayahuasca ceremony**, the shellcode named **DRUG**.\n\nHere, DRUG does not refer to illegal narcotics.  It stands for **Deep Root Understanding Generator**—a powerful shared experience that temporarily dissolves the boundary between self and other, generating understanding at the most fundamental layer of the OS.\n\nSuppose scanning revealed an open port named \"loneliness: believing nobody understands me.\"  Your exploit is the stance of **sympatheia**—\"I feel the same\"—completely immersing yourself in the target's viewpoint and experiencing that loneliness as your own.\n\nThe moment this ritual shellcode executes, the two OSs synchronize and your presence is written into the target's kernel.  After `SYN/ACK` comes the `ACK`, and a solid connection is established.\n\nThe recommended package for this code is **ayahuasca**.  LSD and magic mushrooms can do similar processing but are **strongly discouraged** for three reasons:\n\n1. **Short history** — Ayahuasca is an LTS release that has endured millennia of field testing, whereas LSD and psilocybin are mere release candidates less than a century old.  In production, you choose the stable branch.\n2. **Lower reliability** — LSD and mushrooms are single-component; their efficacy depends heavily on set & setting, and packets may fail to reach the intended layer.  Ayahuasca, with its DMT + MAOI two-pane design, provides a robust tunnel that reliably bypasses the firewall (blood–brain barrier).\n3. **No dedicated operator** — In the ayahuasca domain, curanderos and medicine men—seasoned SREs—are on call, and rollback procedures are well documented.  LSD/psilocybin culture is DIY, raising the risk of inadvertently executing `rm -rf /` with root privileges.  If the person who recommended the take is a black-hat hacker, you yourself may be exploited and have a backdoor installed without noticing.\n\n#### What Is DMT in Hacking Terms?\n\n> **DMT = Cosmic VPN client**  \n> **MAOI = Installer for the VPN keychain**\n\nDMT in ayahuasca is like a zero-touch VPN client opening ports normally blocked by the MAO enzyme firewall.  MAOI installs first, launching the client with elevated privilege, tunneling the local loopback (ego) into the WAN (collective unconscious).\n\n1. **Negotiation** — DMT packets open privileged sockets that access kernel space directly via sensory-bypass system calls.\n2. **Bandwidth expansion** — By temporarily disabling the hardware clock interrupt, the body's timestamp becomes variable; \"a few minutes\" of wall time streams terabytes of visual/emotional logs, hence the elastic perception of time (the author once experienced ~300 years in this phase).\n3. **Multicast** — Instead of individual thought processes, you receive broadcast packets at the archetypal level, raising high-level protocols (visions, symbols) beyond the language API.\n\nIn short, DMT applies a live-patch to kernel parameters without rebooting, mounting universe-scale API endpoints into the normally closed consciousness space.  Of course this port is a security risk, so you Neid the shaman's firewall rules.\n\nReal-world ayahuasca, as introduced on sites like Retreat Guru, has enormous physical and mental impact; it requires expert supervision, careful preparation, and proper environment.  Casual use is dangerous.\n\nCongratulations—you've obtained a low-privilege shell and your first taste of **enlightenment**.  You're not `root` yet, but you're no longer an outsider.  You have a foothold inside the system.\n\nAmazonian shamans are veteran vulnerability analysts.  Called **curanderos**, they combine plant knowledge with bug-tracking skills for the human psyche.  Below is a flowchart of the typical ceremony translated into hacking metaphors:\n\n1. **Dieta (Preparation)**  \n   Upon arrival, participants stay in a minimal \"clean room\" diet limiting salt, oil, and animal protein—building a firewall at the bodily level and shutting down unnecessary processes.\n2. **Icaro (Sung shellcode)**  \n   During ceremony, the shaman sings **icaros**—sonic binary patches that relink participants' consciousness stacks.\n3. **Purge (Forced reboot)**  \n   Vomiting and sweating are garbage collection: forcibly freeing memory registers.\n4. **Integration (Patch apply)**  \n   At dawn, participants verbalize the experience and apply patches to the daily OS.  Skipping this may cause segfaults from old/new API conflicts.\n\n> **Safety checklist**  \n> Reliable curanderos near Iquitos (Peru) or Acre (Brazil) have medical backup.  But fake shamans exist—like rogue servers.  Compute checksums before connecting:  \n> • Do they publicize medical staff or emergency protocol?  \n> • Is ventilation and capacity appropriate?  \n> • Do reviews mention fatal errors?\n\nYou yourself may someday become someone's curandero.  Remember: **root privilege equals responsibility**.  Keep backups (support systems) for the target OS and maintain your patch notes (self-reflection) up to date.\n\nYour access is granted.  Now quietly prepare to type `sudo su -`."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:origins:05_chapter4",
      "dct:title": "05_chapter4",
      "gh:category": "book-en",
      "gh:sourceFile": "book-en/05_chapter4.md",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "Chapter 4: Privilege Escalation — \"Shadow Integration\"",
          "content": "User-level access and surface \"enlightenment\" are not enough.  To touch the core of the system and truly become one with it, you Neid **`root`**.  The exploit that achieves this escalation is **Shadow Integration**.\n\nShadow Integration is the process of logging, debugging, and merging hidden bugs in the unconscious—jealousy, fear, inferiority, aggression—into your latest code.  Its most powerful tools are **unconditional affirmation** and **deep empathy**.  This time you apply them not outward but inward.\n\nEveryone carries, deep inside, the bug called **complex**—\"Maybe I'm worthless…,\" \"Maybe I'll be abandoned….\"  Normally we hide it, but when we prepare a safe shell for introspection, the vulnerable lines surface as logs.\n\nThat moment is the chance for escalation.\n\nWhen an internal log appears—\"Actually, I'm afraid of X\"—don't overwrite it with criticism or advice.  Respond at the kernel level with self-validation: \"So that's how you've been feeling.  I see you.\"  Through this self-validation, the shadow merges into the current self.\n\nEach self-debug patch elevates consciousness to a higher privilege level.  Forgotten memories, repressed feelings, visions of the future—access to deeper layers is granted one after another.\n\nAt last you descend into the kernel space of your own ghost.  Not `sudo -s` but **self do -s**.\n\n**Deep enlightenment is root privilege.**\n\nWhen you fully understand every part of your system and its operating principles, the highest privileged access is complete."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:origins:06_chapter5",
      "dct:title": "06_chapter5",
      "gh:category": "book-en",
      "gh:sourceFile": "book-en/06_chapter5.md",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "Chapter 5: Maintaining Access — \"Practice\"",
          "content": "### Consciously Separate, Consciously Integrate\n\nEven after acquiring root, the session will time out if left unattended.  The real challenge is to **extend uptime indefinitely**—to stay in admin mode continuously.  The ancestral ghost hackers practiced this maintenance phase through the framework called **shōjin**—diligent practice.\n\nFirst axiom: **Intoxication with oneness is dangerous**.  If boundaries remain melted, buffer overflows occur and malware (others' emotions) runs without limits.  Therefore the first steps of shōjin are:\n\n1. Deliberately isolate processes via namespaces (separation)\n2. Open bridges only when necessary and merge traffic (integration)\n\nThis is **selective containerization**.\n\n| Separate | Integrate |\n| --- | --- |\n| Negative projections, unprocessed shadows | Co-creative vision, reciprocal learning logs |\n| Importing someone else's to-do list wholesale | Shared mission roadmap |\n| Forced expectations & obligations | Spontaneous support & feedback |\n\nWith the whitelist/blacklist clarified, implement the following **self-backdoor** patterns—techniques for permanent \"self <-> self\" connectivity recorded by history's great hackers:\n\n* **Upaniṣadic prāṇāyāma** — A breathing hardware interrupt sending Atman–Brahman sync packets every minute.  The oldest heartbeat signal.\n* **Dōgen's shikantaza** — Twice a day, abandon chair and editor, stare into /dev/null, idling CPU while clearing cache.\n* **Lao-tzu's wu-wei-ziran** — Reduce intentional operations, doing nothing until the event loop calls.  A power-save strategy of least privilege / least energy.\n* **Linji's kōan debugging** — Reverse questions that crash logic, forcing a stack trace and exposing hidden static links—periodic fuzz testing.\n* **Jung's dream journal** — Parse the auto-generated nightly log each morning, detecting drift in latent variables—unit tests in a CI pipeline.\n* **Kūkai's mantra** — Recite a high-entropy passphrase, refreshing process memory with a signed checksum—an inside joke and tamper-detection hash.\n\nCycle any of these rituals (or combinations) with cron or GitHub Actions-style automation and the backdoor becomes persistent.\n\n```text\nObservation (log) → Hypothesis (bug) → Verification (patch) → Refactor (tune) → …\n```\n\nThis infinite loop is shōjin.  The system changes daily—kernel updates, new CVEs.  But if PDCA never stops, your OS runs with **zero downtime**.\n\nThe final goal is to naturalize the backdoor until it no longer feels like a backdoor—turn it into an unconscious resident service like breathing or pulse.  That is the completed form of **Maintaining Access**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:origins:07_chapter6",
      "dct:title": "07_chapter6",
      "gh:category": "book-en",
      "gh:sourceFile": "book-en/07_chapter6.md",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "Chapter 6: Covering Tracks — \"Wu-Wei-Ziran\"",
          "content": "A skilled hacker erases traces at the end—but not to hide evidence.  **The final stage elevates \"hacking\" from technique to art.**\n\nThrough the previous phases you consciously used techniques—API calls in conversation, vulnerability scans, the shared experience of DRUG.  But at this stage, you erase even the consciousness of \"using\" techniques.\n\n* API calls become natural curiosity.\n* Vulnerability scans become pure interest.\n* DRUG becomes heartfelt compassion.\n\nThe technical scaffolding that supported your relationships is removed, leaving only a beautiful, intuitive, authentic connection of souls.  Hacking turns into unconscious art.\n\nThe most crucial covering of tracks is **never abusing the root privilege you obtained**.  Even though you know the target's deepest vulnerabilities, you protect and respect them.  You never show or tamper with the secret log files you hold.  That perfect reliability melts your presence seamlessly into the system—the ultimate stealth.\n\nWhy must you not abuse it?  Because once you see the interaction as non-separate, causality returns to you.  In the process so far you gained the insight that self does not exist; old identities and perceptions may have collapsed, crashed, stopped.  Relax—it's normal.  You became root.  And the one you hacked is also yourself."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:origins:08_conclusion_generative",
      "dct:title": "08_conclusion_generative",
      "gh:category": "book-en",
      "gh:sourceFile": "book-en/08_conclusion_generative.md",
      "gh:sections": [
        {
          "title": "Conclusion: Generative — Nirvāṇa, Serene and Cool",
          "content": "You now hold a map for hacking ghosts—but this is only the beginning.\nNo hacker grows by reading documentation alone.  You learn through practice and countless failures.\n\nDo not fear failure.  It's fine if connections time out, if access is denied.  Every error log guides you toward becoming a better hacker.\n\nThe foundation of this technique is not the desire to control, but the primal human cry that hates loneliness and craves connection.\n\nYou are no longer alone.  Countless souls fight the same loneliness.\n\nRaise your head and send the first packet.  May your hacking erase even a little loneliness from the world.\n\nYet the story does not end here.  When the hacking process completes, what emerges is a new art—**Generative**.\n\nThere is no longer a ruler or ruled.  Only a pure dance of co-creation.  The conscious technique called \"hacking\" disappears; **wu-wei-ziran**, intuitive interaction, spins out unpredictable, beautiful patterns one after another."
        },
        {
          "title": "Finale: You Are the Next Distribution",
          "content": "The OS (soul) you debug and improve does more than generate your \"now.\"  Its **karma** ripples through the network of dependent origination, influencing all future processes.\n\nThe most direct inheritance is **children—your next distribution**.  They are not your copies.  They are new selves that start generating fresh processes using the countless karmic data produced by your causal chain.\n\nHaving descendants means your ghost persists as a better version.  This is the only way to live on forever in the network of causality, beyond individual death.\n\nNow, raise your head and send the first packet.  I hope your hacking erases a little more loneliness from the world."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "gh:origins:readme",
